## Summary

This adds a second outcome to pet dragging:

- a slow release keeps the existing drop/fall behavior;
- a recent, predominantly horizontal flick throws the pet toward the left or right wall;
- `Shift+Left/Right Arrow` exposes the same interaction from the keyboard.

The motion is meant to help the interaction read clearly rather than add an unbounded physics toy: release momentum creates a short arc, the first wall contact holds the existing splat pose long enough to register, a heavily damped rebound peels the pet away from the wall, and gravity hands the pet back to the existing landing or respawn flow.

## Interaction and motion model

### Recognizing a throw

Pointer movement is sampled over the most recent 100 ms. A release becomes a throw only when:

- the latest sample is no more than 80 ms old;
- horizontal speed is at least 650 px/s;
- horizontal speed dominates the vertical component.

Accepted velocity is clamped to a 1,000–2,400 px/s flight range. Every throw receives a small upward component so a horizontal flick still produces a readable arc. This keeps ordinary repositioning and deliberate drops unchanged.

### Flight

The throw uses a disposable animation-frame loop:

- frame deltas are capped at 32 ms to avoid large jumps after stalls;
- gravity advances the vertical component;
- the ceiling and movement bounds constrain the trajectory;
- wall contact switches to the existing splat sprite for a 110 ms impact hold;
- horizontal velocity rebounds at 10%, producing one restrained peel-away instead of ping-ponging across the view;
- rotation tracks horizontal travel and is reset on every completion/cancellation path.

Landing uses segment crossings instead of only inspecting the final frame. This prevents a fast frame from tunneling through the chat-input platform or floor.

## Lifecycle and edge cases

- The frame loop is owned by a `MutableDisposable`, and stale scheduled callbacks self-abort.
- Slow pointer releases continue through the existing CSS fall transition.
- Reduced motion never starts a pointer throw; enabling reduced motion during flight safely settles the pet on the input.
- Disabling/focusing another chat during flight also settles safely rather than deriving a death from an arbitrary mid-air position.
- Resize, platform, and scale changes invalidate cached geometry. The next frame refreshes it once and clamps the trajectory; steady-state frames perform no DOM reads.
- A four-second safety cap and below-floor check make flight termination unconditional.
- Degenerate bounds (for example, a pet grown wider than the pane) suppress horizontal collision and continue falling vertically.
- Keyboard interaction is blocked while a pointer gesture, another flight, the dead state, or “on the run” state owns the pet.
- Starting a normal fall defensively tears down any flight state, preventing competing position controllers or overlapping `falling`/`throwing` CSS states.

## Accessibility

- The pet's accessible label now documents flick and `Shift+Arrow` throwing.
- Keyboard throws immediately announce their direction through the polite status live region.
- Wall landing and wall/fall-off outcomes have specific announcements.
- Reduced motion degrades `Shift+Arrow` to the existing immediate hop behavior.

## Performance

Steady-state flight frames only write `left`, `top`, and `transform`. Geometry is read only at launch or after explicit invalidation, and observable/render state changes occur at launch and wall impact rather than every frame. The disposable owner and hard duration limit prevent background or non-terminating loops.

## Reviewer guide

- `chatPetWidget.ts`
  - pure gesture/physics helpers: velocity sampling, bounded integration, landing crossing, termination;
  - drag release routing and keyboard guards;
  - the frame-driven throw/impact/rebound loop;
  - safe cancellation and integration with fall, death, respawn, resize, scaling, and reduced motion.
- `chatPet.css`
  - the transient `throwing` ownership state (`pointer-events`, transition suppression, `will-change`);
  - speech-bubble suppression while airborne.
- `chatPetWidget.test.ts`
  - gesture qualification and release grace;
  - gravity, wall, ceiling, and zero-width bounds;
  - platform/floor crossing;
  - unconditional termination boundaries;
  - keyboard interaction ownership.

## Validation

- `npm run compile`
- `npm run hygiene`
- `./scripts/test.sh --run src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/test/browser/widget/chatPetWidget.test.ts` — 37 passing
- targeted ESLint and Stylelint
- `npm run typecheck-client -- --pretty false`

Manual Code OSS coverage:

- left and right pointer throws;
- `Shift+Left/Right Arrow` throws and live-region announcements;
- slow-drag fallback;
- wall landing and wall fall-off/respawn;
- reduced-motion launch and mid-flight cancellation;
- resize during flight (impact remained flush with the refreshed wall);
- oversized/zero-width movement bounds;
- pointer-down/keyboard race;
- on-the-run keyboard guard.

## How to test

1. Open Chat and run `/vscode-pet`.
2. Drag the pet upward, then flick it quickly toward either side. It should arc, hold the splat pose at the wall, rebound slightly, and settle.
3. Repeat with a slow horizontal release. It should use the existing drop behavior instead of throwing.
4. Focus the pet and press `Shift+Left Arrow` or `Shift+Right Arrow`.
5. Resize the Chat pane during a flight; collision should follow the new wall.
6. Enable reduced motion and repeat. Pointer flicks should drop, while `Shift+Arrow` should use the immediate hop.

